# `regression::glm_predict`

Applies a fitted generalised linear model to new predictor rows, on the scale of the mean or of the linear predictor.

## Signature

```cpp
Series regression::glm_predict(const regression::GLMFitResult &fit, const Series &x, regression::GLMPredictionType type = regression::GLMPredictionType::Mean, Config cfg = {});
Series regression::glm_predict(const regression::GLMFitResult &fit, const Series &x, const Series &offset, regression::GLMPredictionType type = regression::GLMPredictionType::Mean, Config cfg = {});
```

## What It Does

Forms the linear predictor η = Xβ from the stored coefficients and, for `Mean`, passes it through the inverse link, μ = g⁻¹(η), so that the answer is an expected count, a probability or an expected value on the scale of the response. With `Linear` it returns η itself. The intercept convention comes from the fit: give only the original predictors. The second overload adds a known offset, one value per new row, to η before the inverse link; a model fitted with an offset must be predicted with one, or the rate it learned is applied to a unit of exposure it was not measured on.

Call it for a point prediction of the mean at new rows; it returns no interval. For an interval, build it on the `Linear` scale from `std_errors` and `covariance` and transform the endpoints through the inverse link, which keeps a probability inside (0, 1).

## Parameters

`fit`
: A completed result from `glm_fit`.

`x`
: The new rows. For a one-predictor fit, `[m]` is accepted. For several predictors use `[m, p]`; a single new observation is `[1, p]`. A matrix whose width equals `fit.k_params` is read as the full design with its leading column of ones.

`offset`
: A `Series` of length `m`, the offset of each new row. Only in the second overload.

`type`
: `GLMPredictionType::Mean` (the default) or `GLMPredictionType::Linear`; see [`GLMPredictionType`](GLMPredictionType.md).

`cfg`
: Computation configuration for the returned `Series`.

## Returns

A one-dimensional `Series` with one value per new row.

## Errors

Throws `glm_predict: fit.coefficients is undefined` for a default-constructed fit, `glm_predict: offset must have one value per row of X` when the offset length differs from the number of rows, and an error naming the expected width when `x` has neither `num_predictors` nor `k_params` columns.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: expected counts

Fit counts against dose and predict the expected count at three new doses.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const auto doses = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, {3}, as_f64());
  out.writeln("expected counts", reg::glm_predict(fit, doses).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: the linear scale

The same predictions as log-counts, and the exponential that recovers the means.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const auto doses = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, {3}, as_f64());
  const auto eta = reg::glm_predict(fit, doses, reg::GLMPredictionType::Linear);
  out.writeln("linear", eta.items<double>(), " exp", eta.exp().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: several predictors, one new row

With two predictors a single new observation is a `[1, 2]` matrix.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  std::vector<double> flat;
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) flat.push_back(0.5 * (i + 1));
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) flat.push_back((i % 4) * 0.7);
  const auto x2 = pluss::make_series(flat, {2, 10}, as_f64()).transpose(0, 1);
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x2, options);
  const auto row = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{3.0, 1.4}, {1, 2}, as_f64());
  out.writeln("expected count at (3.0, 1.4)", reg::glm_predict(fit, row).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: a rate with an offset

A fit with the log of the exposure as its offset predicts a count only once the new exposure is supplied. The program predicts for the same dose over one and over four units of exposure.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto exposure = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  options.offset = exposure.log();
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const auto dose = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{2.0, 2.0}, {2}, as_f64());
  const auto time = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 4.0}, {2}, as_f64());
  out.writeln("expected counts over 1 and 4 units", reg::glm_predict(fit, dose, time.log()).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: probabilities from a logistic fit

For a binomial fit the default prediction is a probability; the linear scale is the log-odds.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Binomial;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const auto hours = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 3.0, 5.0}, {3}, as_f64());
  out.writeln("probabilities", reg::glm_predict(fit, hours).items<double>());
  out.writeln("log-odds     ", reg::glm_predict(fit, hours, reg::GLMPredictionType::Linear).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 5: the offset overload on the linear scale

The overload that takes an offset also accepts the prediction type. With `Linear` it returns the log-count including the offset, and the exponential of that is the expected count.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto exposure = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  options.offset = exposure.log();
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const auto dose = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{2.0, 2.0}, {2}, as_f64());
  const auto offset = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 4.0}, {2}, as_f64()).log();
  const auto eta = reg::glm_predict(fit, dose, offset, reg::GLMPredictionType::Linear);
  out.writeln("linear scale", eta.items<double>(), " exponential", eta.exp().items<double>());
  out.writeln("mean scale  ", reg::glm_predict(fit, dose, offset).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 6: an interval on the linear scale

A prediction has no interval of its own, but one is built from the result: the variance of the linear predictor at a new point is c₀₀ + 2x c₀₁ + x² c₁₁ from `covariance`, the interval is η ± 1.96 times its square root, and its endpoints go through the inverse link. For the logistic fit the endpoints become probabilities, and they stay inside (0, 1).

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1}, {10}, as_f64());
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Binomial;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  const std::vector<std::vector<double>> cov = fit.covariance.host<double>();
  const double at = 3.0;
  const auto b = fit.coefficients.items<double>();
  const double eta = b[0] + b[1] * at;
  const double half = 1.959964 * std::sqrt(cov[0][0] + 2.0 * at * cov[0][1] + at * at * cov[1][1]);
  const auto inverse_logit = [](double v) { return 1.0 / (1.0 + std::exp(-v)); };
  out.writeln("probability at 3", reg::glm_predict(fit, pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{at}, {1}, as_f64())).items<double>()[0]);
  out.writeln("95% interval", inverse_logit(eta - half), inverse_logit(eta + half));
}
```

### Example 7: the errors

The errors of prediction: a fit that was never made, an offset of the wrong length and rows of the wrong width.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace reg = pluss::subsp::stats::regression;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  const auto x = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0, 4.5, 5.0}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto y = pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 0, 2, 3, 2, 5, 4, 8, 9, 14}, {10}, as_f64());
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      const std::string text(e.what());
      out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
    }
  };
  reg::GLMOptions options;
  options.family = reg::GLMFamily::Poisson;
  const auto fit = reg::glm_fit(y, x, options);
  report([&] { reg::glm_predict(reg::GLMFitResult{}, x); });
  report([&] { reg::glm_predict(fit, x, pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1.0, 2.0}, {2}, as_f64())); });
  report([&] { reg::glm_predict(fit, pluss::make_series(std::vector<double>{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, {2, 3}, as_f64())); });
}
```

## See Also

[`glm_fit`](glm_fit.md) produces the fit, [`GLMPredictionType`](GLMPredictionType.md) chooses the scale, and [`GLMFitResult`](GLMFitResult.md) holds the coefficients applied.

